x on `email`.** Wrapping the column in a function forces a full scan of `users`, because the planner can't use a plain B-tree index on `email`. You have three fixes:\n   - Add an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));` The query then works as written.\n   - Use a case-insensitive type, such as `citext` in Postgres or a `_ci` collation in MySQL. Then you can write `WHERE u.email = '...'` against a plain index.\n   - Lowercase emails when you store them. Then compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` directly.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` pulls every column from both tables.** That has several costs:\n   - It sends wide rows over the wire.\n   - It prevents index-only scans.\n   - It returns duplicate column names (`id` and others appear in both tables), which can break clients that map columns by name.\n   - It silently changes when columns are added.\n\n   List only the columns you need.\n\n3. **`orders.user_id` may have no index.** Postgres doesn't create one automatically for foreign keys, so check. Without it, each matching user means a scan of `orders`. Add `CREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);`. A composite index such as `(user_id, created_at)` helps if you later sort or filter by date.\n\nThere is also a result-size risk, though it isn't an index problem. If a user has many orders, the query returns all of them, with the user columns repeated on every row. Add a `LIMIT` or pagination, or fetch the user first and then page their orders.\n\nIf `users.email` has a unique constraint on the raw value, it won't prevent duplicates that differ only by case. That's a correctness issue worth deciding on when you pick a fix for #1.\n\nI only read the query. I haven't seen the schema or run `EXPLAIN`, so I don't know which indexes exist. If you share the schema or the `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` output, I can say which of these matters most.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}